1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,020
Marco's branch arrived as one large commit:
213 files and about 46,000 new lines.

2
00:00:06,340 --> 00:00:08,649
Most of those lines are not code.

3
00:00:08,969 --> 00:00:10,382
They are evidence:

4
00:00:10,382 --> 00:00:14,227
test questions,
saved search results, and grades.

5
00:00:14,467 --> 00:00:17,713
Almost all of it is about one question:

6
00:00:17,713 --> 00:00:20,159
when a caller asks something,

7
00:00:20,159 --> 00:00:24,038
does search hand the agent
the right passages?

8
00:00:24,358 --> 00:00:26,235
Marco turned three dials.

9
00:00:26,555 --> 00:00:28,518
How the documents are cut.

10
00:00:28,838 --> 00:00:30,462
How the search is run.

11
00:00:30,782 --> 00:00:32,914
And how results are ordered.

12
00:00:33,234 --> 00:00:37,291
He also added a way
to check the spoken answers.

13
00:00:38,333 --> 00:00:41,299
First, how do you score a search?

14
00:00:41,619 --> 00:00:47,033
You ask a question,
take the top 5 passages it returns,

15
00:00:47,033 --> 00:00:49,887
and a person grades each one:

16
00:00:49,887 --> 00:00:57,085
0 if it does not help, 1 if it partly helps,
2 if it answers the question.

17
00:00:57,333 --> 00:01:04,841
Precision at 5 is the share of the five
that help at all, here 2 out of 5.

18
00:01:05,161 --> 00:01:14,026
MRR looks only at the first helpful passage:
it is at rank 2, so the score is one half.

19
00:01:14,346 --> 00:01:16,538
Rank 1 would score 1.

20
00:01:16,783 --> 00:01:20,033
NDCG rewards putting
the best passages first.

21
00:01:20,353 --> 00:01:21,869
It scores our order,

22
00:01:21,869 --> 00:01:27,173
then scores the best possible order
of the same passages, and divides.

23
00:01:27,493 --> 00:01:29,200
1 means perfect order.

24
00:01:29,450 --> 00:01:32,582
All three numbers depend on the grades.

25
00:01:32,902 --> 00:01:34,627
No grades, no score.

26
00:01:35,667 --> 00:01:37,493
The first dial is chunking.

27
00:01:37,813 --> 00:01:39,869
Marco compared three chunkers.

28
00:01:40,189 --> 00:01:42,781
A is the original one from episode 2.

29
00:01:43,101 --> 00:01:46,698
B never merges sections
with different headings,

30
00:01:46,698 --> 00:01:50,445
and repeats the table rows
that name the programs.

31
00:01:50,765 --> 00:01:53,633
C makes small passages for searching,

32
00:01:53,633 --> 00:01:57,586
each linked to a larger parent
passage for context.

33
00:01:57,834 --> 00:02:03,016
To compare them fairly,
he built three separate test libraries,

34
00:02:03,016 --> 00:02:08,280
called staging knowledge bases,
and left the main one untouched.

35
00:02:08,600 --> 00:02:14,776
Library C is the one his voice demo
points at, the ID we met in episode 0.

36
00:02:15,017 --> 00:02:18,737
He asked the same 25 questions to all three,

37
00:02:18,737 --> 00:02:23,980
took the top 5 each time,
and graded all 375 passages by hand.

38
00:02:24,300 --> 00:02:28,339
The team's notes call
these grades provisional.

39
00:02:29,383 --> 00:02:33,282
We reran Marco's
own scoring script on his grades.

40
00:02:33,602 --> 00:02:37,916
The original chunker A has
the best precision and NDCG.

41
00:02:38,236 --> 00:02:44,203
The hierarchical chunker C finds
the first helpful passage a little sooner.

42
00:02:44,523 --> 00:02:46,354
B is lowest on all three.

43
00:02:46,674 --> 00:02:50,397
All three take
about four tenths of a second.

44
00:02:50,633 --> 00:02:55,090
Question by question,
A was best 9 times and C 7 times.

45
00:02:55,410 --> 00:02:59,014
6 were ties,
and B was never best on its own.

46
00:02:59,334 --> 00:03:05,049
For 3 questions, none of the three libraries
found anything helpful,

47
00:03:05,049 --> 00:03:09,251
although related words
do appear in the documents.

48
00:03:09,571 --> 00:03:13,540
Cutting the text differently
did not fix them,

49
00:03:13,540 --> 00:03:17,077
so these are the questions to study next.

50
00:03:17,316 --> 00:03:22,317
That matters, because on this branch
B becomes the default chunker.

51
00:03:22,637 --> 00:03:28,655
With 25 questions and provisional grades,
these gaps are small and could change.

52
00:03:28,975 --> 00:03:31,740
But nothing here shows B is better,

53
00:03:31,740 --> 00:03:37,426
and that is worth discussing
before the main library is rebuilt with it.

54
00:03:38,467 --> 00:03:41,302
The second dial is the search itself.

55
00:03:41,622 --> 00:03:47,612
In feedback mode, retrieve first searches
with the caller's exact question.

56
00:03:47,932 --> 00:03:52,594
Then it reads what came back,
and may plan one more search.

57
00:03:52,914 --> 00:03:58,287
It runs it, keeps a new passage
only if it matches the question,

58
00:03:58,287 --> 00:04:00,386
and merges the two lists.

59
00:04:00,634 --> 00:04:05,258
An extra search comes from words
that are actually there:

60
00:04:05,258 --> 00:04:11,912
a heading or phrase in the first results
that shares real words with the question.

61
00:04:12,232 --> 00:04:16,605
If none qualifies,
it uses the caller's own key words,

62
00:04:16,605 --> 00:04:22,194
plus a variant from a short fixed list,
like lonely to companionship.

63
00:04:22,514 --> 00:04:29,059
For "my mother needs help showering",
one run added the search "bathing in bed,

64
00:04:29,059 --> 00:04:33,865
in the tub or shower",
a line from the personal care page.

65
00:04:34,100 --> 00:04:36,472
This is what model-free means:

66
00:04:36,472 --> 00:04:40,900
no AI model writes the extra searches,
only fixed rules.

67
00:04:41,220 --> 00:04:45,379
Another mode,
called intent, asks a language model,

68
00:04:45,379 --> 00:04:51,495
but the README warns that the team's account
may not be allowed to call it.

69
00:04:51,734 --> 00:04:53,353
To merge the lists,

70
00:04:53,353 --> 00:04:58,039
each passage gets points
from every list it appears in:

71
00:04:58,039 --> 00:04:59,829
more for a high rank,

72
00:04:59,829 --> 00:05:04,008
and the first search
counts one and a half times.

73
00:05:04,328 --> 00:05:07,837
A passage that both searches agree on rises.

74
00:05:08,157 --> 00:05:11,391
This is called reciprocal rank fusion.

75
00:05:11,634 --> 00:05:16,045
We checked one consequence
with Marco's own merge code.

76
00:05:16,365 --> 00:05:21,705
A passage found only by the extra
search scores at most 1 over 61.

77
00:05:22,025 --> 00:05:27,787
The tenth result of the first search
scores 1.5 over 70, which is more.

78
00:05:28,107 --> 00:05:33,448
So today the extra search can reorder
what the first search found.

79
00:05:33,768 --> 00:05:37,413
It cannot add a new passage to the top five,

80
00:05:37,413 --> 00:05:41,473
unless the first search
returned fewer than five.

81
00:05:41,793 --> 00:05:47,080
The code's comments suggest
new passages were meant to get in,

82
00:05:47,080 --> 00:05:50,748
so this is worth a conversation with Marco.

83
00:05:51,783 --> 00:05:56,204
Marco saved a run of 40 everyday
home-care questions,

84
00:05:56,204 --> 00:05:58,373
with and without feedback.

85
00:05:58,693 --> 00:06:01,870
Extra searches were used for 18 of them.

86
00:06:02,190 --> 00:06:07,820
For 23 of the 40, the top five passages
came out exactly the same.

87
00:06:08,066 --> 00:06:09,479
Feedback costs time.

88
00:06:09,799 --> 00:06:14,458
The typical search went
from about 0.43 to 0.51 seconds,

89
00:06:14,458 --> 00:06:20,031
and the slowest tenth from about half
a second to about one second.

90
00:06:20,266 --> 00:06:22,303
And here is the key gap:

91
00:06:22,303 --> 00:06:26,461
none of the 788 passages
in that run were graded.

92
00:06:26,781 --> 00:06:30,820
So the run tells us
about speed and behaviour,

93
00:06:30,820 --> 00:06:34,946
but not whether feedback
finds better passages.

94
00:06:35,183 --> 00:06:38,206
The planner has changed since that run.

95
00:06:38,526 --> 00:06:44,013
We replayed today's version offline
on the same 40 first searches:

96
00:06:44,013 --> 00:06:46,840
it adds a search for 17 questions,

97
00:06:46,840 --> 00:06:51,329
almost all of them rewrites
of the caller's own words.

98
00:06:51,649 --> 00:06:55,745
And feedback is now the default
in the voice app.

99
00:06:56,783 --> 00:06:59,588
The last dial checks answers, not passages.

100
00:06:59,908 --> 00:07:02,287
A test call produces two transcripts.

101
00:07:02,607 --> 00:07:08,712
A script compares the assistant's answer
with a reference answer written by a person:

102
00:07:08,712 --> 00:07:11,944
shared words, required
and forbidden phrases,

103
00:07:11,944 --> 00:07:15,535
and whether the agent
actually looked anything up.

104
00:07:15,783 --> 00:07:20,007
Shared words are measured
with cosine similarity again,

105
00:07:20,007 --> 00:07:22,080
this time over word counts.

106
00:07:22,400 --> 00:07:23,634
That has a trap:

107
00:07:23,634 --> 00:07:30,036
an answer with one wrong dollar amount
shares almost every word with the right one.

108
00:07:30,356 --> 00:07:34,841
Marco's notes say it plainly:
similarity is not correctness.

109
00:07:35,161 --> 00:07:38,155
A person still has to check the facts.

110
00:07:38,400 --> 00:07:43,375
Right now there is one reference answer,
marked as a starter.

111
00:07:43,695 --> 00:07:47,197
Real testing needs 20 to 30 checked ones.

112
00:07:48,233 --> 00:07:52,816
Episode 2 ended with a table
that hid its program names.

113
00:07:53,136 --> 00:07:56,084
Marco's branch attacks it three ways.

114
00:07:56,404 --> 00:08:02,965
The new chunker fixes it: we checked,
and the 1,090 dollar chunk now says JACC.

115
00:08:03,285 --> 00:08:06,794
A new extractor was written for that PDF,

116
00:08:06,794 --> 00:08:10,902
but on the real file it fails
on all four pages,

117
00:08:10,902 --> 00:08:15,438
because it finds nine columns
where it expects seven.

118
00:08:15,758 --> 00:08:19,971
And retrieve now drops every chunk
from that table

119
00:08:19,971 --> 00:08:22,835
that the extractor did not verify.

120
00:08:23,155 --> 00:08:27,799
So for now,
the table does not reach the agent at all.

121
00:08:28,033 --> 00:08:29,590
On the voice side,

122
00:08:29,590 --> 00:08:34,667
a phone number is saved
only after the agent reads it back

123
00:08:34,667 --> 00:08:36,767
and the caller says yes.

124
00:08:37,087 --> 00:08:42,713
And empty search results can no longer
be presented as evidence.

125
00:08:42,950 --> 00:08:45,914
What is open: grade the feedback run,

126
00:08:45,914 --> 00:08:50,559
ideally without knowing
which mode produced which passage.

127
00:08:50,879 --> 00:08:54,892
Decide whether the merge
should let new passages in.

128
00:08:55,212 --> 00:08:57,834
Fix the extractor for the real PDF.

129
00:08:58,154 --> 00:09:01,109
Decide which library is the live one,

130
00:09:01,109 --> 00:09:04,783
since none of this
is in the main library yet.

131
00:09:05,103 --> 00:09:10,705
And note that the branch puts
the AWS account number and library IDs

132
00:09:10,705 --> 00:09:15,729
into a public repository,
which the setup guide had kept out.

133
00:09:15,966 --> 00:09:18,632
Three questions to test yourself.

134
00:09:18,952 --> 00:09:24,252
Why can a search with a perfect
MRR still have a low precision?

135
00:09:24,572 --> 00:09:28,770
Why can today's extra
search reorder the top five

136
00:09:28,770 --> 00:09:30,311
but not add to it?

137
00:09:30,631 --> 00:09:36,076
And why is high word similarity
not proof of a correct answer?

138
00:09:36,316 --> 00:09:40,120
Next, episode 3 goes inside a single search,

139
00:09:40,120 --> 00:09:45,566
and episode 4 follows a whole phone call
through the voice app.

